/*! \page developer_backend_complex_page Complex Numbers

\ref developer_backend_device_page (Prev) &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;
\ref developer_backend_field_page  (Next) &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;
<BR>

Some mathematical operations performed by PSCF require use of
complex numbers, and arrays of complex numbers. The use of complex 
numbers in PSCF arises primarily from the use of Fourier transforms 
in the psuedo-spectral solution of the MDE, since the discrete 
Fourier transform of a real-valued field defined on the nodes of 
a mesh yields an array of complex-valued numbers. 

\section developer_backend_complex_types_sec Complex Number Types

Throughout PSCF, template specializations that use the CUDA and C++ 
backends use different data types to represent individual complex numbers.
This difference is a result of the fact that these different backends use 
different external libraries to perform fast Fourier transform (FFT) 
operations, and these different FFT libraries use different data types
for complex numbers. 

The C++ backend uses the FFTW library. The version of FFTW used by
PSCF uses double precision real numbers and a data type named
fftw_complex to represent a complex number. The typename fftw_complex
is defined within the fftw3.h header file for FFTW to be alias for an 
array containing two double precision numbers, representing the real 
and imaginary parts of a complex number.

The CUDA backend instead uses the cufft library that is provided 
as part of the NVIDIA CUDA toolkit, which performs FFT operations 
on a GPU.  The cufft library can perform transforms using either 
single or double precision for real numbers, but PSCF uses double 
precision by default. The types used by cufft for real and complex 
numbers are referred to throughout the PSCF source code by typename 
aliases cudaReal and cudaComplex that are defined in the file
src/pscf/backend/cuda/cudaTypes.h. When PSCF is configured to use
double precision, cudaReal is simply an alias for double.  The 
typename cudaComplex is an alias for a struct with fields named 
x and y that are each of type cudaReal, and that represent the
real and imaginary parts of a complex number, respectively.

\section developer_backend_complex_aliases_sec Aliases for Complex Types

The backend identifer classes Pscf::CPT and Pscf::CUT each define a 
pair of typename aliases named Real and Complex, which act as aliases
for the real and complex number tyeps uses by the FFT library used 
by the associated backend.

The definition of class Pscf::CPT thus reads, in part
\code
namespace Pscf {

    class CPT {
       
        static const BackendId id = BackendId::Cpp;

        using Real = double;
        using Complex = fftw_complex;

        // ...
        
    };

}
\endcode
where fftw_complex is the complex number type used by the FFTW
fast Fourier transform library.

The corresponding definition of Pscf::CUT reads, in part
\code
namespace Pscf {

    class CUT {
       
        static const BackendId id = BackendId::Cuda;

        using Real = cudaReal
        using Complex = cudaComplex;

        // ...
        
    };

}
\endcode
where cudaReal and cudaComplex are aliases used throughout 
the PSCF source code for the real and complex types used by 
the cufft library.

Within the scope of a class or function template that takes 
a template parameter T that represents a backend identifier 
class, the names 
\code
   typename T::Real
\endcode
and 
\code
   typename T::Complex
\endcode
can thus be used to refer to the real and complex types used 
by the associated FFT class.  Unfortuntely, the "typename" 
keyword is generally required in such type declarations in
order to explicitly tell the compiler that the names T::Real 
and T::Complex represent typenames rather than names of class 
members. 

To avoid the need to repeatedly use the "typename" keyword,
some such class templates define private or protected aliases 
such as
\code
   using RealT = typename T::Real;
   using ComplexT = typename T::Real;
\endcode
that can be used within the scope of that class template.

